::ENG4_U02_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: In Homer's The Odyssey, Odysseus spends years trying to return home after war. The epic uses a journey pattern involving obstacles, recognition, and the question of what home means after long absence. Contemporary passage: After three years working overseas, Amara returned to a changed city and realized that coming home was not the end of the journey but the last test of it. Which classic pattern influences the contemporary passage? {
~A. A courtroom confession#This is not the classic pattern.
=B. The difficult homecoming after long absence#Correct. Both texts use return as a test.
~C. A comic mistaken identity scene#That is not central here.
~D. A recipe for travel#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.11] Classic context: The Odyssey asks what home means after long absence. Contemporary passage: Amara expected the streets to recognize her, but new towers had risen where the market used to lean into the river. What transformation occurs? {
~A. The classic pattern disappears entirely.#The homecoming pattern remains.
~B. Amara becomes Odysseus literally.#She is a contemporary character, not Odysseus.
~C. The city is unchanged.#The city has new towers.
=D. The heroic return becomes a modern experience of changed place and identity.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Contemporary passage: Her mother opened the door, studied her face, and said, "You have brought the distance with you." Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Amara's mother recognizes that Amara's time away has changed her.#Correct. This preserves the idea.
~B. Amara's mother says the trip never happened.#This contradicts the line.
~C. Amara's mother refuses to open the door.#She opens it.
~D. Amara's mother only comments on luggage size.#The line is figurative.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Classic influence means: {
~A. Two texts must have identical plots.#Influence can include transformation.
~B. A newer text cannot change anything from the source.#Adaptations often change sources.
=C. A later text is affected by an earlier text's patterns, ideas, or structures.#Correct
~D. A classic text must be ignored.#The classic pattern matters.
}

::ENG4_U02_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Contemporary passage: Only then did Amara understand that coming home was not the end of the journey but the last test of it. What is the functional significance of this final sentence? {
~A. It says the journey never mattered.#It makes the journey meaningful.
=B. It reframes homecoming as an emotional test rather than simple arrival.#Correct
~C. It proves Amara never traveled.#She worked overseas.
~D. It removes the influence of the classic pattern.#It strengthens the homecoming pattern.
}
